WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.060
In this video, you review the virtual machine auto balance add-on, which uses the Kubernetes Descheduler to move virtual machines away from busy hosts.

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:17.230
On the Add-ons page, the virtual-machine-auto-balance add-on is enabled. It runs the Kubernetes Descheduler.

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:19.900
Open the add-on,

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:24.180
and select Show Configuration to view its settings.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:30.710
The descheduler policy is in the valuesContent field, further down in the YAML.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:37.770
The valuesContent field holds the descheduler settings and its policy.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:41.050
The descheduler runs every five minutes.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:45.050
Each run evicts at most five pods from a host.

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:54.930
The label selector limits evictions to virt-launcher pods, so only virtual machines move. Each eviction becomes a live migration.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:58.690
System namespaces are excluded from eviction.

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:08.510
The LowNodeUtilization plugin compares reserved CPU and memory. A host below thirty percent of both counts as underutilized.

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:18.740
A host above fifty percent counts as overutilized. The descheduler evicts virtual machines from it, so they move to underutilized hosts.

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:21.170
Close the panel.

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:27.470
Open the Hosts page to compare these thresholds with the reserved resources.

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:37.360
Every host reserves more than thirty percent of its CPU. No host is underutilized, so the descheduler moves nothing.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:49.042
The descheduler checks the hosts every five minutes and live-migrates virtual machines only when a host crosses the thresholds. Next, you connect the cluster to SUSE Observability.
